      "program_name": "Behavioral Health Services Act (BHSA) Housing Interventions and capital development RFP, 2026-2029 Integrated Plan",
      "administering_agency": "San Luis Obispo County Behavioral Health Department",
      "funding_type": "grant",
      "typical_award_size": "Housing Interventions budget $6,930,885 (FY 2026/27), $6,792,267 (FY 2027/28), $6,656,422 (FY 2028/29), 30% of BHSA. Funds about 107 PSH beds, 86 assisted living and board and care beds, 10 time-limited beds, FSP housing, landlord mitigation and participant assistance. Capital development: yes, one project in Year 1 via an RFP for a minimum of two units at up to $450,000 of BHSA per unit, braided with other funds, available by June 30, 2027, with by-right provisions required. Behavioral Health Bridge Housing beds start Year 2.",
      "per_unit_cap": "$450,000 BHSA capital per unit (state cap); 25% of housing funds maximum for capital.",
      "affordability_requirement": "BHSA-eligible adults with serious mental illness or substance use disorder experiencing or at risk of homelessness; half of housing funds for chronic homelessness.",
      "eligible_project_types": [
        "supportive housing"
      ],
      "application_cycle": "rolling",
      "next_deadline": "Capital development RFP expected in FY 2026/27 (units by June 30, 2027). Plan submitted to DHCS March 2026, final June 30, 2026; annual updates follow.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "true",
      "layering_notes": "San Luis Obispo County's Department of Social Services Homeless Services Division (HSD, Devin Drake, Director; George Solis, Principal Administrative Manager; HSDgrants@co.slo.ca.us) runs one combined annual Action Plan NOFA each fall for CDBG, HOME, ESG, HHAP, PLHA, Title 29 and County General Fund Support on the Neighborly portal, and is the Continuum of Care collaborative applicant and administrative entity (CA-614). The Department of Planning and Building runs Title 22 land use incentives and assesses the new Title 31 in-lieu fee; the Behavioral Health Department holds BHSA; the Housing Authority of San Luis Obispo (HASLO) and its San Luis Obispo Nonprofit Housing Corporation, Paso Robles Housing Authority and Peoples' Self-Help Housing are the main developers; and the private nonprofit San Luis Obispo County Housing Trust Fund is the local CDFI gap lender. The Urban County covers the unincorporated area plus Arroyo Grande, Atascadero, Morro Bay, Paso Robles, Pismo Beach and San Luis Obispo (Grover Beach is outside). The County repealed its inclusionary ordinance on June 7, 2022 and replaced it in October 2025 with a voluntary Regional Housing Incentive Program and Regional Housing Fund. slocounty.ca.gov opens directly but rate-limits bursts (HTTP 429); Legistar web API is not enabled for the County. The capital set-aside is tiny (two units), so BHSA matters mainly as an operating and rental subsidy source for PSH units in a larger project. Transitions-Mental Health Association is the County's main housing partner. Contact Behavioral Health about committing operating subsidies to units in a Homekey+ or LIHTC project.",
